From: Lars Wikman <lars@underjord.io>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: qcom: rcg2: keep a floor clock's configuration when it gives the rate

When a parent's rate changes, clk_change_rate() calls set_rate on each
child with the rate its current configuration gives from the parent's
new rate. A floor RCG looks that rate up rounding down, and it is rarely
a table frequency: GPLL0 on msm8937 recalculates to 799999792 Hz, so
SDCC2's 50 MHz is 49999987 Hz, and the floor lookup takes the 25 MHz
entry. The next cascade takes 20 MHz, then the 400 kHz XO entry, where
it stays, with the core still believing it runs from GPLL0.

The GPU's gfx3d_clk_src sets its parent's rate (CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT), and
asks for GPLL0 at its nominal 800 MHz, so every GPU runtime suspend and
resume cascades through GPLL0's children. On the watch that left the
Wi-Fi's SDIO bus at 400 kHz within seconds of boot, a ~1 Mbit/s ceiling.

Asked for the rate its configuration already gives, a floor RCG now
keeps that configuration.

Signed-off-by: Lars Wikman <lars@underjord.io>
---
--- a/drivers/clk/qcom/clk-rcg2.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/qcom/clk-rcg2.c
@@ -689,6 +689,18 @@ static int clk_rcg2_set_gp_rate(struct c
 static int clk_rcg2_set_floor_rate(struct clk_hw *hw, unsigned long rate,
 				   unsigned long parent_rate)
 {
+	/*
+	 * A parent's rate change re-sets each child at the rate its
+	 * configuration gives from the parent's new rate. That rate is
+	 * rarely a table frequency (a PLL that's a few hundred Hz off its
+	 * nominal rate makes 50 MHz 49999987 Hz), and the floor lookup
+	 * would take the entry below it, so every such cascade would slow
+	 * the clock another step. The configuration already gives this
+	 * rate: keep it.
+	 */
+	if (rate == clk_rcg2_recalc_rate(hw, parent_rate))
+		return 0;
+
 	return __clk_rcg2_set_rate(hw, rate, FLOOR);
 }
 
